    "['delete set protocols [parameter1] [parameter2] [parameter3]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'no network 172.16.1.0 0.0.0.255 area 0', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'no network 172.16.1.0 0.0.0.255 area 0', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the translation of the Juniper OSPF network removal commands to Cisco IOS equivalent:\\n\\n##\\nno network 172.16.1.0 0.0.0.255 area 0\\nno network 172.17.1.0 0.0.0.255 area 0\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Cisco IOS:\\n1. The \"no\" prefix is used to remove configuration\\n2. Wildcard masks are used instead of prefix lengths\\n3. The area ID format is the same (area 0)\\n4. These commands would be entered in router ospf configuration mode', 'trans_template': 'no network 172.16.1.0 0.0.0.255 area 0'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'no network 172.17.1.0 0.0.0.255 area 0', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'no network 172.17.1.0 0.0.0.255 area 0', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the translation of the Juniper OSPF network removal commands to Cisco IOS equivalent:\\n\\n##\\nno network 172.16.1.0 0.0.0.255 area 0\\nno network 172.17.1.0 0.0.0.255 area 0\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Cisco IOS:\\n1. The \"no\" prefix is used to remove configuration\\n2. Wildcard masks are used instead of prefix lengths\\n3. The area ID format is the same (area 0)\\n4. These commands would be entered in router ospf configuration mode', 'trans_template': 'no network 172.17.1.0 0.0.0.255 area 0'}]]": 1,
    "['network [parameter2]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'no network 172.16.1.0 0.0.0.255 area 0', 'parent_command': ['no network 172.16.1.0 0.0.0.255 area 0'], 'root': 'no network 172.16.1.0 0.0.0.255 area 0', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nno network 172.16.1.0 0.0.0.255 area 0\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- The Juniper `delete` command removes an OSPF network statement\\n- In Cisco:\\n  - `no` is used instead of `delete`\\n  - The network format changes to: `network <address> <wildcard> area <area>`\\n  - The wildcard mask 0.0.0.255 is the inverse of /24 (255.255.255.0)\\n  - The area is specified directly as 0 (Cisco uses standard area numbering)', 'trans_template': 'no network 172.16.1.0 0.0.0.255 area 0'}]]": 1,
    "['network [parameter2]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'no network 172.17.1.0 0.0.0.255 area 0', 'parent_command': ['no network 172.16.1.0 0.0.0.255 area 0'], 'root': 'no network 172.16.1.0 0.0.0.255 area 0', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nno network 172.17.1.0 0.0.0.255 area 0\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- The Juniper `delete set protocols ospf area 0 network 172.17.1.0/24` command removes an OSPF network statement\\n- In Cisco IOS, this translates to `no network` with:\\n  - The network address (172.17.1.0)\\n  - Wildcard mask (0.0.0.255 for /24)\\n  - Area number (0)\\n- The Cisco command uses inverse mask (wildcard) instead of prefix length\\n- The command is prefixed with `no` to remove the configuration, just like `delete` in Juniper', 'trans_template': 'no network 172.17.1.0 0.0.0.255 area 0'}]]": 1,
